-- title:
--   Representability of the rigidified-pair functor on each edge chart
-- statement:
--   The theorem is stated inside the full Čerednik–Drinfeld frame at a uniformising prime $r$; the hypotheses are grouped below, each group being named, and the content of the longer groups is summarised where the text says so.
--
--   **Arithmetic data.** Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$ (`hrr`), a nonzero level $N$ with $r \nmid N$, $\bar r \nmid N$ and $N$ squarefree (`hrN`, `hrbarN`, `hN`).
--
--   **The coefficient rings.** A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi$ (`hπ`), $\mathcal O$ complete for the $\pi$-adic topology (`hcomplete`), residue ring of cardinality $r$ (`hres`), and $(r) = (\pi)$ (`hunr`); a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is the fraction field of $\mathcal O$. A characteristic-zero domain $Onr$ over $\mathcal O$ with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $Fr$, such that $Onr$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $Onr$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo that ideal (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial of positive degree over $Onr$ has a root modulo that ideal (`hOnr_closed`), and $Fr(x) \equiv x^{r}$ modulo that ideal (`hFr`). A homomorphism $vdet : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) with `hvdet`: $vdet(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\pi^{n}$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **The indefinite quaternion algebra and its moduli.** Rationals $a,b$ with `hB`: $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and for every finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$ the completion $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b] \otimes \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ lies above $r$ or above $\bar r$. A maximal order $\Lambda$ (`hΛ`: an order, maximal among the orders containing it); an element $\mu_\Lambda \in \Lambda$ with $\mu_\Lambda^2 = -(r\bar r)$ (`hμΛ`) and a map $star_\Lambda$ on $\Lambda$ with $\mu_\Lambda\, star_\Lambda(x) = \bar x\, \mu_\Lambda$ (`hstarΛ`); $2$ invertible in $\mathcal O$ (`h2`). A scheme $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with point rule $pt$ for fake elliptic curves with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ data, such that `h𝒳` holds: $pt$ is invariant under isomorphisms, compatible with base change, and bijective on geometric points over algebraically closed fields, and $(\mathcal X, pt)$ has the universal property of a coarse moduli space. An auxiliary level $n \geq 3$ with $r \nmid n$, $\bar r \nmid n$ and $\gcd(n,N) = 1$; a scheme $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with point rule $ptF$ for curves with full level-$n$ structure, `hM` asserting that $ptF$ is isomorphism-invariant, compatible with base change, and bijective on $S$-points for all $S$; a group $G$ acting by $\rho$ on $M$ with labels $\chi : G \to \Lambda$ satisfying `hρ` (the action is over the base, realises level twisting by $\chi(g)$, and $\chi$ is multiplicative, unital, surjective and injective modulo $n$). A forgetful morphism $p : M \to \mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ (`hp`), invariant under $\rho$ (`hρp`), compatible with the point rules (`hp_pt`).
--
--   **Hecke and Atkin–Lehner structure.** For each prime $\ell$ distinct from $r$ and $\bar r$, a coarse moduli scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ for curves with extra level $\ell$ with point rule $ptT$ and `h𝒴`, together with two morphisms $d_0\ell, d_1\ell : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ over the base (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) realising respectively the forgetting of the $\ell$-structure (`hd₀`) and the passage to an $\ell$-isogeny quotient (`hd₁`). Endomorphisms $ar, arbar$ of $\mathcal X$ over the base (`harf`, `harbarf`) realising the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ on points (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   **The definite quaternion algebra and the uniformising group.** Rationals $a_1,b_1$ with `hdef`: $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$ and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ ramified exactly at the places above $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda_1$, an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders of relative index $N$) with $R_1 \leq \Lambda_1$; an idelic unit $n_1$ in the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$ (`hn₁`) with `hS₁`: the order $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ is Eichler of level $Nr$; an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\iota_0$ of the definite algebra into $M_2(K_0)$; a height-one prime $v$ containing $r$. The subgroup $\Gamma t$ of the unit group, characterised by `hΓt` as the group of units lying in the local unit box of $R_1$ at every place other than $v$; families $s$ and $sf$ indexed by the primes $\ell$ away from $r,\bar r$ with `hs`: $sf_\ell$ agrees with $s_\ell$ at all places not above $r$, is $1$ at the places above $r$, the product of the diagonal idele of $\ell$ with $sf_\ell^{-1}$ lies in the level Hecke set of $(\Lambda_1, R_1\cap n_1R_1n_1^{-1})$ at $\ell$ if $\ell \mid N$ and in the prime Hecke set otherwise, and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell) = \ell$; $\Gamma t_\ell = \Gamma t \cap s_\ell \Gamma t s_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`); a unit $wbar$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\Gamma t$ (`hwbar`).
--
--   **The special fibre dictionary.** All rational integers lie in $\Lambda$ (`hΛℤ`); a coordinate map $coord : \Lambda \to \mathbb Z_{r^2}^{\,2}$ satisfying `hcoord` (additive, sending $1$ to $(1,0)$, multiplicative for the twisted rule involving the Witt-vector Frobenius, injective, with dense image, and compatible with reduced traces). A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ over $Onr/(\pi)$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ of height $4$ (`hX₀`) over that ring, formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of dimension $2$ along the identity of $A_0$, and `hA₀`: $\theta_0$ are formal coordinates for the group law of $A_0$ and the $\Lambda$-action is given through $coord$ by the action and the uniformiser of $X_0$. Families $e, e'$ of endomorphisms of $A_0$ indexed by $\Gamma t$, with $e\gamma$ over the base (`he`), and degrees $deg$. The clauses `hE1`, `hE1mul`, `hE1sc`, `hE1'`, `hE2`, `hE3`, `hE4` (summarised here) assert: each pair $(e\gamma, e'\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{deg\gamma}$ preserving the level structure; $e$ is multiplicative up to composing with multiplication by powers of $r$; rational integral scalars $\gamma$ give scalar $e\gamma$ up to powers of $r$, and conversely $e\gamma$ scalar up to a power of $r$ forces $\gamma$ scalar; for every injective ring map $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $X_0$-action and uniformiser into $M_2(K_0)$ there is $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that each $e\gamma$ is induced on formal coordinates by an element $\varepsilon$ of that centraliser with $E_0\varepsilon = r^{k_\gamma} g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$; each full level-$n$ structure on $A_0$ carries a labelling $lab : \Gamma t \to \Lambda$ with $e\gamma$ acting on it as $lab\gamma$, multiplicative and scalar-compatible modulo $n$; and every $r$-power-degree self-isogeny pair, preserving the level, of a fake elliptic curve over an algebraically closed field pulled back from $A_0$ comes from some $e\gamma$ up to powers of $r$. An order $R_2 \leq R_1$ (`hR₂o`) with $r$-power denominators for $R_1$ (`hR₂r`) and a family $\hat e$ of endomorphisms of $A_0$ over the base (`hê`), subject to `hE5a`–`hE5e` (summarised here): $\hat e x$ is additive on points, commutes with the $\Lambda$-action and preserves the level; $\hat e 1$ is the identity; $\hat e$ is multiplicative, sends integers to the corresponding $\Lambda$-action, and satisfies $\hat e(\bar x)\circ \hat e(x) = \mathrm{nrd}(x)$; $e\gamma$ and $\hat e x$ agree up to powers of $r$ when $x = r^{k}\gamma$; and the matrix dictionary of `hE2` holds simultaneously for the $e\gamma$ and for the $\hat e x$, with $E_0\varepsilon = r^{k_x} g\,\iota_0(x)\,g^{-1}$.
--
--   **The Drinfeld moduli package.** A ring map $\iota : \mathbb Z_{r^2} \to Onr$; a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $Onr/(r)$ which is special for the reduction of $\iota$ (`hΦ`) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`); a moduli package $MD$ over $Onr$ which is a Zariski sheaf (`hMD`); a family $\eta$ sending rigidified data over a ring $B$ with $r$ nilpotent to $MD$-points, with `hη` (three clauses): $\eta$ separates admissible rigidified data exactly up to isomorphism, is compatible with base change along ring maps over $Onr$, and is surjective after passing to a standard Zariski cover; an injective ring map $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $\Phi$-action and uniformiser into $M_2(K_0)$ whose image is commensurable with $M_2(\mathcal O)$ in the precise two-sided sense of `hE₀`.
--
--   **The abstract Deligne-datum comparison.** A family $eD$ sending points of $\mathrm{ModuliPackage.G}\ \mathcal O\ MD$ over a Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent to pairs consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $Onr \to B$ and a Deligne datum over $B$, natural in $B$ (`hnatD`), bijective (`hbijD`), whose first component is the structure map of the point (`hfstD`), transforming the $\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$-action on $G$-points into the twisted action on pairs (Frobenius twist by $-vdet(g)$ on the first component, pullback of the datum by $g^{-1}$) (`hGLD`), sending $\pi$-translates to the Frobenius twist by $1$ of the first component with unchanged datum (`hPiD`), and such that $\pi$-translates always exist (`hPiexD`). The boundedness clause `hbdd`: for every pair of full $\mathcal O$-lattices $M' \subseteq M$ in $K_0^2$ with $\pi M \subseteq M'$ there is $N_\gamma$ such that for every Artinian local Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with algebraically closed residue field and $\pi$ nilpotent, and every $G$-point $y$ over $B$ whose Deligne datum lies in the edge chart $(M',M)$, the point $y$ is represented by an admissible rigidified object $t$ with $t.n \leq N_\gamma$.
--
--   **Reduction comparison.** A ring map $\kappa : Onr/(\pi) \to Onr/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps (`hκ`), an integer $n_0$ and a series $\beta_0$ exhibiting an isogeny of height $4n_0$ from $\Phi$ to the base change of $X_0$ along $\kappa$ (`hβ₀`). A full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$.
--
--   **Atkin–Lehner data on $A_0$.** A curve $A_{0w}$ with morphisms $aw, aw'$ exhibiting it as the $\bar r$-Atkin–Lehner quotient of $A_0$ (`hALw`), morphisms $bw, bw'$ forming an isogeny pair of degree $r^{kw}$ (`hBSw`) preserving the level (`hBSwlev`), and `hJOINTw`, which extends the matrix dictionary of `hE2` by an element $\varepsilon_w$ of the centraliser inducing $aw$ followed by $bw$ on formal coordinates with $E_0\varepsilon_w = r^{k_w} g\,\iota_0(wbar)\,g^{-1}$.
--
--   **The endomorphism dictionary $\Xi$.** A family $\Xi$ sending a rigidified curve over a Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, relative to an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : Onr \to B$, to a $G$-point of $MD$, subject to: `hΞleg` (over connected $B$ the structure map of $\Xi$ is a Frobenius twist of $\psi$), `hΞnat` (compatibility with base change along pullbacks of rigidified curves), `hΞiso` ($\Xi$ is unchanged under isomorphisms of rigidified curves admitting the displayed comparison of the two legs up to powers of $r$), and `hΞdef` (over connected $B$ with $r$ nilpotent, for each formal-module structure $(X,\theta)$ on the curve there are $j \leq 1$ and an admissible rigidified object $t$ with $t.X = X$, transported from $\theta_0$ through $\kappa$ and $\beta_0$, with $\Xi$ equal to the triple consisting of the Frobenius twist of $\psi$ by $-j$ and $\eta(t)$). A matrix $g_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and the two equivariance clauses `heqΓ` and `heqW`: over connected $B$, translating a rigidification by $e\gamma$ corresponds to the action of $r^{c}\,g_0\iota_0(\gamma)g_0^{-1}$ on $G$-points for some $c \in \mathbb Z$, and, when $\bar r$ is invertible in $B$, passing to an $\bar r$-Atkin–Lehner quotient with comparison data as displayed corresponds to the action of $r^{c}\,g_0\iota_0(wbar)g_0^{-1}$.
--
--   **The base $C$ and the rigidified-pair point functor.** A Noetherian commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra $C$ with $\pi$ nilpotent (`hCπ`) and an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\chi_C : Onr \to C$; a functor $PR$ on $C$-algebras; a point rule $ptR$ assigning an element of $PR(S)$ to each full level-$n$ curve over $S$ together with a rigidification relative to the structure map induced by $\chi_C$. These are subject to: `hR2` (naturality of $ptR$ along base change of pairs), `hR3s` ($ptR$ is surjective), `hR3i` (over $S$ with no nontrivial idempotents, $ptR$ is injective up to an isomorphism of level structures together with the displayed comparison of rigidifications), `hR1` ($ptR$ is constant on such isomorphism classes); a natural transformation $\theta$ from $PR$ to the Deligne-datum functor $\mathrm{Omega}\ K_0\ \pi$ (`hθnat`) characterised on presentations by `hθ`: $\theta_S(ptR(u,\rho))$ is the pullback along $g_0^{-1}$ of the Deligne-datum component of $eD(\Xi(u,\rho))$; a map $toM$ from $PR$ to points of the pullback of $fM$ along $\operatorname{Spec} C \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, natural (`hR0`) and compatible with $ptF$ (`hR4`); $PR$ a Zariski sheaf in the form `hsh` (unique glueing over standard open covers); `het'` (unique lifting of points of $PR$ along square-zero surjections, relative to a prescribed lift of the $M$-point) and `hred` (the same relative to reduction modulo $\pi$); and the degree strata: schemes $Xs_d$ with morphisms $\xi_d$ to that pullback which are locally of finite presentation (`hξ`) and formally unramified (`hunrξ`), a point rule $pts$, and a rule $ptX$ producing points of $Xs_d$ from pairs whose rigidification has degree index $d$ over bases where $\pi$ is zero, subject to `hs0` (such points only exist when $\pi$ vanishes in $T$), `hs1` ($toM \circ pts$ is the composite with $\xi_d$), `hs2` and `hx2` (naturality), `hx1` ($pts \circ ptX = ptR$), `hx3` (surjectivity of $ptX$), `hx4` (an explicit criterion for equality of two $ptX$-points) and `hs4` (a point of $PR$ coming from a pair lies in the image of $pts$ exactly when the pair admits a rigidification of degree index $d$ with the same $ptR$-value).
--
--   **The standard edge.** A matrix $g_\pi \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ with $g_\pi = \operatorname{diag}(\pi, 1)$ (`hgπ`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $\gamma \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ there exist a scheme $X$, a morphism $fX : X \to \operatorname{Spec} C$ which is locally of finite type, and a family $e$ assigning to every Noetherian $C$-algebra $S$ that is also an $\mathcal O$-algebra in a scalar tower with $C$, and to every $S$-point of $X$ over $\operatorname{Spec} C$ (that is, every morphism $\operatorname{Spec} S \to X$ whose composite with $fX$ is the structure morphism), an element of $PR(S)$, such that the following three statements hold.
--
--   First, $e$ is natural: for $\varphi : S \to S'$ a $C$-algebra map and points $y$ over $S$, $y'$ over $S'$ with $y'$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}\varphi$ followed by $y$, one has $e_{S'}(y') = PR(\varphi)(e_S(y))$.
--
--   Second, $e_S$ is injective for every such $S$: $e_S(y) = e_S(y')$ implies $y = y'$.
--
--   Third, the image of $e_S$ is exactly the subset of $PR(S)$ cut out by the translated edge chart: for every $x \in PR(S)$, there exists a point $y$ of $X$ over $S$ with $e_S(y) = x$ if and only if the Deligne datum $\theta_S(x)$ lies in the edge chart of the pair of full lattices $\bigl(\gamma g_\pi L_0,\ \gamma L_0\bigr)$, where $L_0$ is the standard full lattice $\mathcal O^2$ in $K_0^2$; that is, if and only if for every prime ideal $\mathfrak p$ of $S$ one has $\gamma g_\pi L_0 \subseteq \gamma L_0$, $\pi\,\gamma L_0 \subseteq \gamma g_\pi L_0$, and the line of $\theta_S(x)$, enlarged by $\mathfrak p$ times the whole base-changed lattice, contains neither $1 \otimes v$ for $v \in \gamma L_0 \setminus \gamma g_\pi L_0$ nor $1 \otimes v'$ for $v' \in \gamma g_\pi L_0$ not divisible by $\pi$ inside $\gamma L_0$.
--
--   This is the representability input to the Čerednik–Drinfeld rigidity argument for fake elliptic curves at the uniformised prime $r$: over each translate of the standard edge chart of the formal upper half plane, the functor of rigidified pairs (a fake elliptic curve with full level-$n$ structure together with a rigidification along the special fibre) is cut out by a $C$-scheme locally of finite type, with the chart condition expressed on Deligne data. It is cited by the existence and uniqueness statements [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.FakeEllipticCurve.evenRigidifiedPair_exists_of_rigidifiedToG_of_isUnit_two`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.FakeEllipticCurve.evenRigidifiedPair_exists_of_rigidifiedToG_of_isUnit_two) and [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.FakeEllipticCurve.evenRigidifiedPair_unique_of_rigidifiedToG_of_forall_isIdempotentElem_of_isUnit_two`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.FakeEllipticCurve.evenRigidifiedPair_unique_of_rigidifiedToG_of_forall_isIdempotentElem_of_isUnit_two); no claim is made that $PR$ itself is a scheme.
